<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vanua Levu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vanua Levu is the second-largest island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, lying north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a>, with Labasa and Savusavu as its main towns. In the 19th century its chiefdoms of Macuata, Bua and Cakaudrove were drawn into wars with Bau and with the Tongan noble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enele-maafu">Enele Maʻafu</a>, who extended his power over parts of the island. Under British rule it became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane and copra production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vanua-levu.jpg" alt="Vanua Levu" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fiji_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
